    {
      "id": "ES-C305-nombre",
      "sequence": 4370,
      "title": "el nombre — the word that is also English",
      "headword": "el nombre",
      "romanization": "el nombre",
      "gloss": "the name — Latin nomen, cousin to English name and root of noun and nominate",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:cbd70f7ecd5b835e",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el nombre — \"the name.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin nomen. English name. These are not borrowings in either direction — they are cousins, both descended from the same ancient word, which is why they still agree in sense after several thousand years apart."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin lent the borrowed forms too: a noun is a naming word, to nominate is to name somebody for a post, and renown is being re-named, over and over, by people who were never there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You already say me llamo — \"I call myself.\" El nombre is the thing you call yourself with."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word for the thing your first sentence was already handing over."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el nombre",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el nombre*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el nombre, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el nombre, por favor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "me llamo… ¿y el nombre de usted?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *me llamo… ¿y el nombre de usted?*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Are nombre and name borrowed from each other? (No — cousins, from the same ancient word.) What is renown? (Being named again and again.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C305-apellido",
      "sequence": 4380,
      "title": "el apellido — the name you are called by",
      "headword": "el apellido",
      "romanization": "el apellido",
      "gloss": "the surname — from appellitare, to call on repeatedly; English appeal sits on the same verb",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:49ea5af412a22595",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el apellido — \"the surname,\" the family name."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It comes from Latin appellitare, the repeating form of appellare, \"to call on.\" Not the name you are given — the name you are called by, again and again, by everybody."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has the same verb in appeal (to call on someone) and appellation (what a thing is called)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Worth knowing before anyone asks for it: Spanish speakers usually carry two apellidos, the father's then the mother's. A form with one box for \"surname\" is a form built for a different country."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two halves of a full name now, and a warning about forms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el apellido",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el apellido*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el nombre y el apellido",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el nombre y el apellido*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el apellido, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el apellido, por favor*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does appellitare mean? (To call on again and again.) How many apellidos does a Spanish speaker usually have? (Two.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C305-senor",
      "sequence": 4390,
      "title": "señor — respect made out of age",
      "headword": "señor",
      "romanization": "señor",
      "gloss": "sir, Mr — Latin senior, older; respect built out of age",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:f0a44422830d0535",
