        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "señora — \"madam,\" \"Mrs.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The same senior, turned by the -o/-a switch you have now met three times: bueno/buena, tranquilo/tranquila, and here."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That switch is the single most useful pattern in the language, and you have been collecting it since chapter one without being asked to memorise a rule."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One caution worth having early: señorita exists and marks an unmarried woman. It is going out of use, and señora is the safe address for an adult woman you do not know."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both forms of address, and the -o/-a switch confirmed a third time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
